==Trivia==
*Since [[Merry-Go-Roundcake]] and [[Cannelé Corkscrew]]'s Japanese name refer to amusement park attractions, Six Flans is likely a reference to the amusement park company [[wikipedia:Six Flags|Six Flags]].
**The "Highland" in Six Flans' Japanese name may reference [[wikipedia:Fuji-Q Highland|Fuji-Q Highland]].
*Both Six Flans and [[Mouseyland]], the 36th [[Stories of Legend]] sub-chapter, take place in theme parks with "land" in their Japanese names.
*[[Room for Dessert]]'s Japanese name, Betsubara no Iriguchi (別腹の入り口, "Separate Stomach Entrance"), does not refer to a literal extra stomach; 別腹 ''betsubara'' means an appetite you may have even while full, usually in the context of still wanting dessert after a meal, as though you had a "separate stomach" just for dessert.
*Merry-Go-Roundcake's Japanese name, Baumu Kaiten Mokuba (バウム回転木馬, "Baum Merry-Go-Round"), is a reference to [[wikipedia:Baumkuchen|baumkuchen]], a kind of German spit cake popular in Japan. This sweet's name translates to "tree cake", as the rings that can be seen in its slices resemble a tree's [[wikipedia:Dendrochronology#Growth rings|growth rings]].
*Cannelé Corkscrew is named after [[wikipedia:Canelé|canelé]] (also spelled cannelé), a small French pastry with a crispy, caramelized exterior and a custardy interior.
*Every two stages have a different variant of the same enemy as the boss. This gimmick is similar to [[Truth in Extremes]] and [[Newtown Trench]], except the stages aren't remakes of each other.
**[[Room for Dessert]] and [[Merry-Go-Roundcake]]: [[Le'Behemoth]] and [[Le'Prude]]
**[[Sinister Sorbet]] and [[Sugar Coma]]: [[Divine Cyclone]] and [[The Perfect Cyclone]]
**[[Backstage Baker]] and [[Cannelé Corkscrew]]: [[Marquis Clionius]] and [[Cruel Angel Clionel]]

